in this video I'm going to take a few  minutes to tell you about one of our  sponsors that make the David Pakman show  possible it's a fascinating new project  called Monet and you can find out more  about Monet using the link underneath  the video so Monet is a project that  wants to solve a number of different  problems in the world of blockchain and  peer-to-peer services and these problems  relate to privacy transparency security  and just the efficiency of networks and  first I just want to get everybody up to  speed about some of the background when  we say the peer-to-peer economy we're  talking about people interacting or  doing business directly with one another  without any intermediary someone sells  someone else a car without using a  dealership you send someone a file over  the internet without uploading it  anywhere there's no middleman involved  that's peer-to-peer sometimes we think  of the interactions we have on a site  like eBay as peer-to-peer because we're  buying something from another person or  you use a ride-sharing app where someone  comes and picks you up in their own  personal vehicle these are sort of  peer-to-peer in a sense but they really  aren't because there's still a  centralized intermediary that's  facilitating everything and this is  exactly the space that Monay is dealing  with their goal is to make more of our  communications with one another more of  the business we do with each other more  authentically peer-to-peer without the  need for these middlemen so why would we  want to do that is a good first question  every time we have one of these hubs in  the middle it's a company that's  probably collecting data about us how  much data and what kind of data varies  it can be confusing but it's rarely what  we want can we stop them from collecting  data often you can't if you want to use  their service and you need to use their  service because often there are not good  alternatives often these companies are  hiding things from us there are things  about their platform that they don't  want us to know secrecy that works to  their advantage think about the YouTube  algorithm often these companies are also  compromising the security of our  computers our credit cards our bank  accounts we see this all the time with  the hacks that take place and then of  course when there's someone in the  middle they're taking their cut there's  a monetary cost to the people who are  interacting because in many cases  they're having to  the service that facilitates whatever  the two people are doing which creates a  lot of waste in terms of money a lot of  these issues as Monet sees it could be  solved by having people on the internet  deal with one another in a way that  bypasses the people in the middle  altogether if it would be possible to  have more transactions in cooperation  without somebody in the middle where two  people or a group of people are just  dealing directly with one another  that's what Monet thinks would be  working more in our favor and that's  what their project is all about trying  to get us closer to and many of the  things we do obviously one type of way  that people have tried to decentralize  certain models is what we've seen with  things like Bitcoin where a public  blockchain is used a large decentralized  network that's cryptographically linked  using computers all over the world  anyone can see what's going on in the  network anyone can interact with it it  has many many nodes around the world all  working together at the same time to  keep the network running and this  network might be necessary for something  like a decentralized currency but Monet  sees the public blockchain as a flawed  solution to many of the peer-to-peer  economy needs because not every peer to  peer interaction requires such a massive  network of nodes a lot of the time you  don't need computers on different  contents continents processing your  commands the way you would need with  Bitcoin for example sometimes you just  need to be linked with one other person  or the small group of people that you're  working with the other issue is that  when you interface with a public  blockchain you're still using it  similarly to how you would use many  other centralized online services you're  counting on someone else to do your  computing and to manage your data  so here's Monet's solution here's how  Monet wants to solve the privacy  transparency and security issues of  relying on centralized third parties as  well as just the inefficiencies of  public blockchains Monet offers an  infrastructure whereby two people or a  group of people can establish small  temporary private blockchains where the  only users who are connected are the  users who need to be connected with each  other when you want to start working  with someone else the blockchain is  created  it exists only as long as the users are  wanting to be connected and then it  disappears each node of these  short-lived miniature block chains are  actually mobile devices owned by each of  the users and Monet calls these mobile  ad-hoc block chains and they're building  a free open-source software development  kit which lets developers add them own a  blockchain consensus to their  applications which allows apps to use  these mobile ad-hoc block chains and it  introduces a new paradigm quite frankly  where users can dynamically join or form  small closed off block chains just for  the duration of the transaction it  eliminates the need for servers it's  ephemeral by nature so users aren't  required to stay online to support other  users it can be much more efficient and  much more secure as a way of interacting  and Monet itself is a permanent public  blockchain which provides the  infrastructure services to users and  developers who want to be involved with  these mobile ad-hoc block chains but  Monet is not itself a centralized server  through which end-users would be sending  their communications and Monet even has  potential applications in the gaming  industry most people think of games in  the context of the centralised  client-server model but they often don't  consider the possibilities of  peer-to-peer Monet actually sees  bringing peer-to-peer to gaming as a way  to make game implementation and  maintenance cheaper for game developers  through not relying on servers and  content delivery platforms peer-to-peer  games can also be more stable in the  sense that not having to rely on servers  also means you're not worried about  servers being down which can be a big  problem in the gaming world Monet is  developing a plug-and-play peer-to-peer  solution for game developers that would  also provide a lot of what centralized  servers provide like cheating prevention  and fault tolerance Monet also sees  their mobile ad-hoc blockchains  as being suitable for the transportation  industry so I think of uber uber but  truly peer-to-peer where drivers are  able to make more profit there's not a  big company in the middle of everything  collecting everybody's data creating  other problems there could be use cases  all across the sharing economy  in fact with the potential to  decentralize housing decentralized space  rental decentralized package delivery  and the Monet protocol could be used for  peer-to-peer money lending investing  everyday payments bill sharing a lot of  other just consumer finance applications  and the decentralization could apply to  something as simple as just  communicating right chatting between  phones and in fact they've already  created an app for Android and iOS that  uses the protocol where users can chat  with each other without using any kind  of server in the middle it basically  functions as a messaging or texting app  but can provide more security and  certainly more privacy  so there's lots of different use cases  where this could be applied where a  blockchain itself is replacing what a  server had been doing but with the  benefits of disappearing out of thin air  when you're done using it so that  companies or hackers aren't able to hang  on to all of the data or your personal  information the company behind Monet is  mosaic networks they've been developing  a lot of different innovative software  solutions for years there's a lot of  work that they've been doing building up  to Monet for instance Monet uses a  protocol called Babel which is another  product of mosaic it's what allows  developers to integrate Monet into their  apps mosaic was co-founded by CEO  Giacomo Perini  who was involved in the very early days  of Bitcoin and founded a decentralized  cryptocurrency working closely with  venture capital funds around here in  Cambridge and Boston Massachusetts  Giacomo holds a master's degree in math  and Finance from Columbia mosaics chief  technology officer is Martin era vets  who I actually interviewed on the show a  few weeks ago as part of a sponsored  interview and he created the company's  first products which include babel and  he holds a degree in applied math from  the university of chicago and then you  have Ronan Lynch who's an important  member of the software development team  led a lot of the pioneering work on the  first uses of distributed Ledger's in  financial markets years ago he also has  run several high volume and  operationally complex  trading desks for Scotiabank and the  National Bank of Canada and then John  Knight is another member of the  developer team he has 20 years of  experience in software analysis design  and delivery and has spent the last four  years working for the Cambridge  University Development Trust on a really  innovative app designed to help people  teach English as a foreign language  some of mosaics advisors include MIT  lecturer Mark Stewart day tech executive  Stephanie Lynch Habib finance manager  odd B Hersey and mauro martino who's the  researcher at IBM and a professor here  in boston at Northeastern University so  this is one of the truly more impressive  teams that you see working on blockchain  projects diverse sets of experience  expertise that really culminates in what  they're accomplishing now with Monet so  I've really enjoyed following everything  that's going on with Monet and mosaic  I'm going to continue to do so like I  said if you haven't already check out  our other video on Monet really a  fascinating concept very ambitious goal  that they have but a worthwhile one and  I want to thank Monet for sponsoring  this video I'd love for our viewers to  go learn more about Monet read the white  paper see what they're all about and you  can use the link right underneath the  video to do that

